  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 144 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe upper high remains over the lower MS valley into Friday with a \ncontinued fetch of subtropical moisture feeding northward into NM. \nPWATs look to range from around 0.85 to 1.3 inches with the \ndriest areas remaining in the northwestern to north central \nportions of NM while the lower Pecos River valley holds higher \nvalues. Storms will continue to initiate farther east into Friday,\nincluding more cells over the Sacramentos which will pose \nconcerns for burn scars. Storms will have a harder time moving off\nof the mountains and into the far eastern plains given the south-\nto-north steering flow, but a few mesoscale cold pools may allow \nfor propagation Friday night. This trend will continue on into \nSaturday with scattered to numerous cells initiating over the high\nterrain during the afternoon and filling into adjacent valleys \nand lowlands through the overnight. Both Friday and Saturday \nappear to be the more active storm days with heavy rainfall \npotential for high terrain areas and rainfall rates of 1 to 2 \ninches per hour. The slow struggling expansion of storms in \neastern zones will subdue the heat slightly, but readings will \nstill commonly reach the mid 90's in the eastern plains both \nFriday and Saturday.\n\nUncertainty starts to grow for next week with prognoses indicating that \nthe upper high will push back more centrally over NM Sunday and Monday. \nThis would suggest more subsidence along with a less optimal steering \nflow for subtropical moisture, and ensembles indicate a reduction in \nPWATs over much of AZ and western and northern NM. Blended guidance \nfrom the NBM retains high POPs for Sunday and Monday, and this could be \nan opportunity for fine-tuning and reductions as subsequent \nshifts update the forecast. The upper high looks to move very \nlittle into Wednesday and Thursday with lighter winds and slower \nsteering flow making it difficult for storms to move far off of \nthe high terrain. Therefore, climatologically favored mountain \nranges would fare best with opportunities for rainfall with\nchances decreasing sharply downslope.",
